  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 133 PM CST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nSunshine abounds this afternoon as we are under the influence \nof high pressure to our southeast. A broad ridging and warm air \nadvection pattern will stay in place through Wednesday, so we \nare looking at pretty quiet weather through then. The main story\nwill be above normal temperatures with highs in the 40s and 50s\nand lows staying above freezing. I continued the trend of \ndecreasing RH by less than the 50th percentile of guidance as we\nshould stick with a dry weather pattern.\n\nGoing into Thursday, models are in good agreement that we will \nhave a low pressure system passing through with the low track \nlikely tracking over the Dakotas and moving northeast into NW MN\nand Ontario. There won't be a lot of moisture with this one, \nbut we could see some showers from it. \n\nWe may return briefly to southerly flow and warm air advection \nout ahead of another train of lows that could bring a mixed bag \nof precipitation Friday night and through the weekend. A \ndeepening trough is looking to develop over the eastern Rockies \nand into the upper Midwest, and with that, potential for a \ncouple low pressure systems to deepen roughly over \nColorado/Kansas/Nebraska and move northeast. These will tap into\nsome Gulf and Pacific moisture and bring PWATs to around an \ninch. At this time, precipitation type is somewhat in question \ndue to 1) low track uncertainty and 2) temperature uncertainty \nitself on the northwest side of the low where snow would fall if\nit does. Right now, it looks like there should be enough warm \nair with the first low (Friday night into Saturday) such that \nwe'll probably be dealing with mostly rain. More cold air may \nwrap around a second Saturday-Sunday low and bring some wet \nsnow. We'll have to keep an eye on this because there is \npotential for an fgen band of moderate to heavy precipitation \nrates, and if we can bring in enough cold air, this could \ncertainly be accumulating snow. But again, uncertainty is very \nhigh at this point and taking many possibilities into \nconsideration regarding storm track and thermal profiles, \nchances are higher that we end up with a rain-to-snow setup with\nmainly lighter snow accumulations, most likely in northeast \nMinnesota. The pattern may remain at least somewhat active going\ninto next week.",
